from common import *
it('KERNEL-0114','rule','3.3','Two op forms (GEO-11; J-K15): a transaction takes exactly one of two forms, fixed by its stage registration (§4.1, §14); a transaction supplying both ops and a callback is a programming error caught by an assertion.',['§3.3 bullet Two op forms'],'kernel',['§4.1 stage registration','§14'],['@declaredForm','@callbackForm'],['two op forms','GEO-11','J-K15'])
it('KERNEL-0115','mechanism','3.3','Declared form: KernelTx.ops lists independent ops, none reading another result (S3 carvings, S4 routed arterials, S7 strip lines and furniture, S9 corridorUnion, S11 per-plot footprints, an edit inserted feature). Before applying, the kernel sorts them by a stable key - op kind rank, then the owning entity lineage key (§6.3), then the op index within that entity stream path - so the committed arena is a function of the prior arena and the op SET, not of issue order. Hot-pixel snap rounding is order-independent; attraction, the one order-sensitive rule, is deterministic under the canonical order (op-permutation test §13.1).',['§3.3 Declared bullet'],'kernel',['§6.3 lineage key','@KernelTx.ops'],['§4.2 S3, S4, S7, S9, S11','§7 edit insert','§13.1 op-permutation test'],['declaredForm','canonicalOrder','canonical op order','op kind rank'])
it('KERNEL-0116','mechanism','3.3','Callback form: tx(tx, fn) (§14) hands fn the open overlay; each op fn issues applies at once, in issue order, and later ops read its result (S5c growth queue, S8 bent-cut recursion, S10 plot subdivision are callback-form stages). Issue order is deterministic: a function of the stage inputs, its streams (§6.1) and canonically sorted query results - never slot numbers, worker scheduling or arrival order - and attraction within the sequence follows it (issue-order test §13.2).',['§3.3 Callback bullet'],'kernel',['§14 tx()','§6.1 streams','@queries'],['§4.2 S5c, S8, S10','§13.2 issue-order test'],['callbackForm','tx(tx, fn)'])
it('KERNEL-0117','rule','3.3','No output depends on slot numbers: every kernel tie-break uses lattice positions (y, then x), canonical item keys or entity ids, never a VertexId, HalfEdgeId or FaceId, so an arena compacted on save, one grown through a long session and one merged from pool overlays give identical results for the same next edit (slot-renumbering test §13.1).',['§3.3 bullet No output depends on slot numbers','§3.2 (3)'],'kernel',[],['§13.1 slot-renumbering test','§3.9 save compaction','@merge'],['slot independence'])
it('KERNEL-0118','mechanism','3.3','Work limits instead of clocks (GEO-2): every op charges operation counts to its transaction (points snapped, attraction queries, placement candidates tried, crossings resolved, faces walked, skeleton events); a transaction exceeding its declared workLimit returns E_WORK_LIMIT. Counts are identical on slow and fast machines, so a budget can select a ladder rung without changing what the seed produces.',['§3.3 bullet Work limits','§3.1 E_WORK_LIMIT'],'kernel',['@KernelTx.workLimit','§4.1 declaration'],['@E_WORK_LIMIT','§5.1 ladder rung selection','§12 budgets'],['workLimit','GEO-2','operation counts'])
it('KERNEL-0119','rule','3.3','The commit record lists what the batch created, changed, moved, promoted, demoted and removed per level; it is the input of the batch-local invariants (§3.10), index maintenance, the display list dirty set (§8) and the digest update - at commit the kernel re-hashes the leaves the batch touched (per-level plat digests and entity leaves) and the nodes on their paths to the root (§6.2, whose budget this keeps). Never persisted (holds arena slots). Commit granularity is part of every stage declaration (§4.1), so the about 48 s hazard of per-insertion commits (engineering judge) and its per-batch twin cannot arise.',['§3.3 bullet The commit record'],'kernel',['@CommitRecord'],['@invariants','@indexRebuild','§8 display list','§6.2 digest'],['commit granularity','48 s hazard'])
it('KERNEL-0120','mechanism','3.3','The derivation pool overlays (J-K16): the per-district stages that run in the pool (S10, S10b, S11, S12b; §4.1) use these same transactions on DISTRICT OVERLAYS in five steps (frozen base, one overlay per district, scope check, merge, pool size 0).',['§3.3 The derivation pool overlays'],'kernel (pool scheduling owned by §4.1 / §6)',['§4.1 pool stages'],['§4.1','§6.5 pool-size test','§12.4 pool rows'],['overlay','district overlay','derivation pool','J-K16'])
it('KERNEL-0121','step','3.3','Pool step 1 The frozen base (J-K25): when the eager S10a and S10 shared-edge step (0) have committed (step (0) runs on the generation worker and builds on both sides at once the bands around every L1 edge two overlays face sets share without corridors, step 3, §4.7), step (0) ends as an eager stage does, by rebuilding the static L3 partitions its batches touched (a finer batch writes no coarser index, so L3 is the only level it touches).',['§3.3 pool step 1'],'kernel',['§4.7 S10 (0)','@indexRebuild'],['@overlay'],['frozen base','shared-edge step (0)','J-K25'])
it('KERNEL-0122','rule','3.3','Pool step 1 freeze: at the freeze the generation worker first rebuilds every static partition whose dynamic grid or tombstone set is not empty (none on the one-click path; after edits - an epoch per-district stages, a Re-derive from a stage at or before S10, a verification replay folding either - the partitions the edits left dirty, each holding at most max(256, 1/4 of its static size) grid items) and then asserts every level dynamic grid and every partition tombstone set empty (§3.10). The rebuild is the stage-end rebuild code on live items, changes no output, runs at every pool size, 0 included.',['§3.3 pool step 1'],'kernel',['@indexRebuild','@tombstone'],['@invariants freeze assertion','§6 replay','V06 epochs'],['freeze','freeze rebuild'])
it('KERNEL-0123','budget','3.3','Freeze rebuild costs measured (docs/validation/bench/fixall_kernel_r4_bench.out B): about 2 ms for one district L3 and L4 partitions, about 10 ms for five districts, about 6 ms for the arena-wide L1 partition with one L2 partition, about 59 ms for every partition of a finished CITY. Step (0) own rebuild covers every L3 item it made - at CITY about 9 000 vertices, edges and faces (both sides of about 6 km of perimeter margin band and of the wall band, setback, glacis and tower parts; our count) in about 26 partitions - costing about 2.3 ms at CITY and about 5.8 ms at METROPOLIS (fixall_kernel_r3_bench.out A), carried in §12.4 S10 (0) row.',['§3.3 pool step 1'],'kernel (budget row owned by §12.4)',['bench files'],['§12.4 S10 (0) row'],['freeze cost'],restates='§12.4 S10 (0) row (carried there)')
it('KERNEL-0124','mechanism','3.3','Pool step 1 copy: the generation worker freezes the arena at that version and sends each pool worker one read-only copy as transferred ArrayBuffers - vertex, half-edge, role and face arrays, the role-head and owner-head tables (typed arrays, §2.2), the index home column and every non-empty level static index partitions (L0-L3 at the one-click freeze, L4 still empty; L0-L4 after edits) - so pooled plot lines ending on a wall band inner edge, setback part or margin band, S12b greenery in setback and glacis residue, and a pooled re-derivation kept locked or pinned content are found by the same indexed queries. No SharedArrayBuffer is used (§11.2).',['§3.3 pool step 1'],'kernel',['§2.2 typed arrays','@indexHome'],['pool workers (§4.1)','§11.2 no SharedArrayBuffer'],['frozen copy','ArrayBuffer transfer'])
it('KERNEL-0125','budget','3.3','Frozen copy size: the arena and its tables at most about 12 MB at CITY (§2.2 Size) plus partitions at 38.4 B per item and the home column at 2 B per slot: at the one-click freeze about 60 000-90 000 items (L0-L2 view and step (0) parts; our count, PLAUSIBLE until M1), about 2.2-3.3 MB plus about 0.2 MB home column, so <= about 15 MB per copy and one memory copy of about 1.5 ms per worker (about 4.6 ms for three, measured fixall_kernel_r4_bench.out A); after edits about 20 MB (about 0.5 MB home column; about 2 ms each). §12.4 carries the copies on CITY critical path; §12.8 counts one copy per pool worker.',['§3.3 pool step 1'],'kernel (budgets owned by §12.4, §12.8)',['§2.2 Size'],['§12.4','§12.8','§16 M1 measurement'],['copy size','38.4 B per item','15 MB'])
it('KERNEL-0126','fallback','3.3','Pre-decided fallback if M1 memory measurement (or M4 on the epoch path) exceeds the rung line with these copies: a copy made after edits carries only what a pooled stage may read under the laziness contract (§4.1) - the L0-L2 partitions, every L3 partition (step (0) parts are eager) and the L4 partitions of the districts the pool derives (their kept content); another district footprints are lazy output no pooled stage reads - and then §12.8 pool-shrinking step applies.',['§3.3 pool step 1'],'kernel',['§4.1 laziness contract'],['§12.8 pool-shrinking step'],['copy fallback'])
it('KERNEL-0127','step','3.3','Pool step 2 One overlay per district: a worker derives each district on a fresh overlay over the frozen base; new vertices, half-edge pairs, face records, role rows and the interned handles of entities the district creates go to the overlay own growable arrays in overlay-local slots numbered above the base high-water mark and are remapped at the merge; a change to a base record (a kerb edge split by a plot line, a face ring re-walked) is a copy-on-write record in the overlay; a frozen-partition item the overlay deletes, re-levels or changes is tombstoned in a set the overlay keeps for that partition (the copy own sets are empty, §3.10), consumed by step 4 district-end rebuild; a pooled stage changes only its district L3 and L4 items. Every district starts from the frozen base alone, never another district overlay, so its result is a pure function of the base, its allocation and its own sub-stream, whichever worker runs it.',['§3.3 pool step 2'],'kernel',['@frozenBase','§6.1 sub-stream'],['@merge','@tombstone'],['copy-on-write','overlay-local slots','high-water mark'])
it('KERNEL-0128','step','3.3','Pool step 3 The scope check: a district face set is its L1 faces and every face nested in them; its SCOPE is every face of the set, every edge with a face of the set on both sides, every vertex whose incident faces all lie in the set, and - for a split only (a vertex placed on the edge) - every edge on the set boundary whose other side no overlay writes (a water face, the arena outer ring), so waterfront plot lines may end on their own bank. A write of any kind (split, retype, role row, move, owner change) outside the scope returns E_SCOPE.',['§3.3 pool step 3'],'kernel',['@overlay'],['@E_SCOPE'],['scope','district scope','face set'])
it('KERNEL-0129','rule','3.3','Pool step 3 shared edges: the L1 edges two overlays face sets share are never written in the pool: a shared spine is flanked by corridors whose kerbs each side lines end on (avenue and street-tree rows are Greenery.along records in the planting district own half-corridor strips, not roles on the way, §2.7, §4.9); a shared wall trace gets its band, setback, glacis, tower and gatehouse parts from step (0) before the freeze; every other shared L1 edge without corridors (the settlement perimeter between the districts and the hinterland, any reservation boundary two districts share) gets from the same step a margin band (§4.5), so pooled plot and furlong lines end on the band inner edge inside their own set (J-I9).',['§3.3 pool step 3'],'kernel (bands built by §4.7 S10 (0), §4.5)',['§4.7 S10 (0)','§4.5 margin band','§2.7 Greenery.along','§4.9'],['@scope'],['shared spine','margin band','Greenery.along'])
it('KERNEL-0130','rule','3.3','Pool step 3: the hinterland unit, which the generation worker runs while the pool works, is one more overlay of this kind (§4.1, §4.9). Each overlay also lists the base records it copied; at the merge, two overlays that copied the same base record, or a copied base record the live arena changed after the freeze, fail the pool with E_SCOPE - defence in depth that a test asserts never fires.',['§3.3 pool step 3'],'kernel',['@overlay'],['@E_SCOPE','§13 pool test (never fires)','§4.9 hinterland'],['hinterland unit','copied-record check'])
it('KERNEL-0131','step','3.3','Pool step 4 The merge: the generation worker applies overlays in district lineage order (§6.3): new records appended with a slot remap table (overlay-local slot -> next free arena slot), copy-on-write base records replace originals, references rewritten through the remap, only that district faces re-walked to validate rings and parents. The district L3 and L4 static partitions (built by the worker at district end from the base partition live items, step (0) parts included, and the overlay new ones) travel with the overlay, have item tables rewritten through the remap and replace the base partitions with empty tombstone sets, the home column taking their partition numbers, so the merged arena needs no index rebuild. The overlay added and changed role-head and owner-head keys (kept in its own small tables, consulted before the base) are inserted into the live tables through the remap; the district digest leaves (§6.2) travel with it and the root is finalised once after the last overlay.',['§3.3 pool step 4'],'kernel',['@overlay','§6.3 lineage order'],['§6.2 digest','live arena'],['merge','slot remap table'])
it('KERNEL-0132','budget','3.3','Merge cost is O(overlay size): the about 200 000 records S10-S12b create at CITY merge within the §12.4 8 ms merge row.',['§3.3 pool step 4'],'§12.4 rendering/performance (budget row)',['@merge'],['§12.4 merge row'],['merge budget 8 ms'],restates='§12.4 merge row 8 ms')
it('KERNEL-0133','step','3.3','Pool step 5 Pool size 0: the single-worker configuration runs the same overlay-and-merge path inside the generation worker, district by district in lineage order, so pool sizes 0-3 execute the same code on the same inputs and give identical digests by construction; the golden suite pool-size test (§6.5, §13.2) confirms it on every run.',['§3.3 pool step 5'],'kernel',['@merge'],['§6.5 / §13.2 pool-size test'],['pool size 0'])
add(I)
